hey this is presh talwalkar  i received this question from tim in  singapore  it was given to 10 year olds who had not  yet studied algebra  i slightly edited the problem for  clarity  the total amount of sand contained in  boxes a b and c is 360 grams  each box starts with some unknown amount  of sand and a mathematician decides to  play with the sand  first the mathematician pours one-sixth  of the sand for box a into box b  after that the mathematician pours one  third of the sand in box b into box c  finally the mathematician pours one  fifth of the sand in box c into box a  at the end each box has an equal amount  of sand  how much sand did each box have at the  beginning  now remember this was given to 10 year  olds who had not yet studied algebra so  you should try to solve this problem  without using a system of simultaneous  equations  can you figure it out  give this problem a try and when you're  ready keep watching the video for a  solution  so the way i solve this problem is to  work backwards  we'll create a table with the amounts in  each box  and we'll let the amounts be in grams so  we can drop the units and just focus on  the numbers  so by working backwards that means to  start at the very end  how much sand is in each box at the very  end  the problem tells us each box has an  equal amount of sand at the end and the  total amount of sand is 360 grams  so at the very end each box has 360  divided by 3 which is 120 grams  so we can fill in these amounts in the  table  now let's go one step backwards  how much is in each box before the step  where box c gets poured into box a  well the mathematician pours one fifth  of box c into box a  so if box c minus one fifth of itself  equals 120 then box c must have had 120  times five fourths which equals 150  before the sand was poured out  we can see this by a little bit of  algebra if we have box c times 1 minus 1  5  that's equal to 120  that means box c times four fifths is  equal to 120 so we take the reciprocal  of four-fifths which will be  five-fourths  therefore box c started with 120 times  five-fourths which equals 150.  so we can say that box c had 150.  now since  150 minus 120 is equal to 30 that's how  much was poured from c to a  we can deduce box a must have had 30  fewer grams before the step 120 minus 30  which equals 90.  furthermore pouring from box c to box a  leaves box b unaffected so box b  remained at 120.  now let's go one more step backwards  what happened before box b was poured  into box c  if box b minus one third of itself  equals 120 then box b had 120 times  three halves which equals 180 before  this step  furthermore since this step transferred  60 grams from b to c we know that box c  had 60 fewer grams or 150 minus 60 which  equals 90 before this step  and box a was unaffected in this step so  it remained at 90.  now we go back to the beginning what  happened before box a was poured into  box b  well  if box a minus 1 6 of itself equals 90  then box a had 90 times six-fifths which  equals 108 before the sand was poured  out  furthermore we see that 18 grams was  transferred from a to b  therefore box b must have started with  180 minus 18 which equals 162.  box c was unaffected in this episode  remained at 90.  and there you go  these are the starting amounts  box a had 108 box b had 162 and box c  had 90 grams  so it's a fairly challenging problem to  ask of 10 year olds but it can be solved  with logical considerations and by  working backwards  did you figure it out  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math you can catch me on my blog mind  your decisions if you like this video  you can check out my books which are  listed in the video description and you  can support me on patreon if you have a  math topic you can email me presh  mindyourdecisions.com and you can catch  me on social media either at mind your  decisions or at preshtalwalker
 

